# Burla Worktrees and Dev Clusters

For Burla tasks, never edit the primary checkout. Create or reopen a linked
worktree for the task and edit there.

In this repo the base branch is always `dev`, overriding the "branch off
whatever branch you're on" default. If the checkout is on some other branch when
a task starts, stop and tell Jake before creating the worktree.

One worktree gets one dev cluster. Everything that would otherwise be shared
(docker network, container labels, head port, node ports, head state, relay
subdomain, EC2 node tags) is namespaced by the worktree name automatically, so
several agents can run clusters side by side on one machine. Run
`make cluster-info` to see a worktree's cluster name and dashboard URL; never
assume the head is on port 5001.

Every ad hoc Burla client command in a dev worktree must set both variables
inline: `BURLA_CLUSTER_DASHBOARD_URL="$(make -s cluster-info | awk
'/dashboard/{print $2}')" BURLA_ENVIRONMENT=test <command>`. Never rely on a
prior shell export or automatic cluster resolution; it can select the deployed
test cluster instead of this worktree's cluster.

Start a local cluster from its worktree with `make dev-up` (whole cluster
local, for light work: frontend, dashboard, head/API behavior). It runs
detached in its own OS session, so it survives agent-session cleanup and stays
up until `make dev-down`; never start a cluster as a plain foreground or
backgrounded agent shell, which dies when the agent's terminals are reaped.
Use `make remote-dev` (head local, nodes are real EC2 in the Burla test AWS
account) for real scale or real-VM behavior, or when this machine cannot take
another local cluster. Development happens in AWS; GCP is production-only.
Read the `burla-parallel-dev` skill before starting or stopping a cluster.

A local cluster's job history (`_local_dev_state`) persists across restarts,
so past jobs' dashboard pages keep working after `make dev-down && make
dev-up`; wipe it only with `make dev-clean`. Stop a local cluster with `make
dev-down` (and remote-dev in its own terminal); the cluster cleans up its own
nodes. `make stop-all` is emergency machine-wide cleanup and destroys every
local dev cluster. Never `docker rm` containers by `node_*` name prefix: that
destroys other agents' clusters.
Workers are containers *inside* their node's own docker daemon (each local node
is a privileged fake VM running dockerd), so they die with the node; inspect
them with `docker exec node_<id> docker ps`.

In remote-dev, node VMs run the worktree's current branch from GitHub, so
`node_service` / `worker_server.py` changes must be committed and pushed before
they reach a node. `main_service` changes are always live in both modes.

When the work looks done, ask Jake whether to merge; never merge unprompted.
Once he says yes: push the branch, merge it into `dev`, push `dev`, then
stop the dev-cluster process and remove the worktree and branch. Until he says
yes the worktree stays; if it is dirty, commit the work on its branch with a
clear WIP message rather than discarding it.
